    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>Very often I would like to write something like this:<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inv_gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br>But end up explicitly looping:</div></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inv_gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Since there is </div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> real</span></div></code></div><div>could one get</div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> real</span></div></code></div><span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span></div></div></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">BR,</span></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">Henrik</span></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "3Y4AK_pcVrcJ",
   "from": "Daniel Lee",
   "author": "cab04b8f0759c712",
   "date": "2013-09-27T15:33:31Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Elementwise matrix distributions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><p dir=\"ltr\">We&#39;ve had this one on our TODO list for a long time. The short message is that we&#39;re thinking about it and working on it, but there&#39;s no timeline as to when it&#39;ll get into Stan.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p dir=\"ltr\">The long: it&#39;s tricky. Under the hood, we&#39;re trying to minimize duplication of our code base. If we take the normal distribution as an example, we&#39;ve written a single function that will allow each of the 3 parameters (y, mu, sigma) to be a scalar, an array, a vector, or a row vector. Each parameter can also be declared as data (C++ type double) or as a parameter (C++ type stan::agrad::var). Using template metaprogramming we have a function that will accept all 512 different type configurations. Inside the function, we&#39;re also efficiently calculating the log probability function by vectorizing and not calculating additive constants when they are functions of data and not parameters.</p>\r\n<p>We have some ideas about how to extend it past a vector into matrices, but it&#39;s tricky. So far, we&#39;ve been using arrays, vectors, and row vectors interchangeably. <br></p><p>Here&#39;s an example:</p><p>data {</p>\r\n<p>  real y[3];<br></p><p>}</p><p>parameters {</p><p>  vector[3] mu;</p><p>  row_vector[3] sigma;<br></p><p>}</p><p>model {</p><p>  y ~ normal(mu, sigma);<br></p><p>}<br></p><br></div>So this currently works and it&#39;s the same as saying:<br>\r\n</div>for (n in 1:3)<br></div>  y[n] ~ normal(mu[n], sigma[n]);<br><br></div><br></div>So when we start getting into vectorized matrixes, I think we may need to break backward compatibility for consistency, but I&#39;m not sure. Let&#39;s change the example to:<br>\r\n</div>data {<br></div>  matrix[3,3] y;</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>}<br></div><div>parameters {<br></div><div>  matrix[3,3] mu;<br></div><div>  matrix[3,3] sigma;<br></div><div>}<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>model {<br></div><div>  y ~ normal(mu, sigma);<br>\r\n</div><div>}<br></div><div><br></div><div>I think we can all see that this should be doing:<br></div><div>for (i in 1:3)<br></div><div>  for (j in 1:3)<br></div><div>    y[i,j] ~ normal(mu[i,j], sigma[i,j]);<br><br></div>\r\n<div>What if mu was defined as vector[3] and sigma was defined as row_vector[3]? Then the call should be equivalent to:<br></div><div>for (i in 1:3)<br></div><div>  for (j in 1:3)<br></div><div>    y[i,j] ~ normal(mu[i], sigma[j]);<br>\r\n<br></div>So what should happen to mu if it was defined as &quot;real mu[3]&quot;? In this case it&#39;s ambiguous. Perhaps it shouldn&#39;t be allowed? Or should we define a convention?<br></div><div>If y were actually a 3x4 matrix, should we be clever and take an array to be in the direction that matches? If things have to match dimensions across 2+ directions, I think we may have to not allow mixed types for {array, vector, row_vector} to be consistent, but I&#39;m not sure. <br>\r\n<br></div><div>With regards to your example with all three arguments having the same dimension or a single matrix argument and scalars for the other arguments, it seems straightforward enough, but I think we want to really figure out how to tackle vectorization in any direction. Once we figure it out, I think it won&#39;t take too much time to get everything vectorized appropriately. <br>\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>And this is loosely related to vectorizing multivariate distributions, which is also on our list.<br><br></div>Thanks for being patient. I&#39;ll try to look at it again after v2.0.0 is out.<br><br>\r\n<br></div><div><div><br><br></div><div>Daniel<br><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><br>On Sep 27, 2013 3:32 AM, &quot;Henrik Mannerström&quot; &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">henrik.ma...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br type=\"attribution\">\r\n</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>Very often I would like to write something like this:<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>  X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<br>  mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n  sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> inv_gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br>But end up explicitly looping:</div></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n\r\n\r\n<code><div><span>data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>row </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>col </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n      X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n      mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n      sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> inv_gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Since there is </div><div>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span> vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span> real</span></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n</code></div><div>could one get</div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span> real</span></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n</code></div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);font-family:monospace\"><br></span></div></div></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">BR,</span></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">Henrik</span></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
