     1,
     "There&#39;s an in-between solution I indicate inline below.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Sep 27, 2013 3:32 AM, &quot;Henrik Mannerstr�m&quot; &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">henrik.ma...@gmail.com</a> &lt;mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">henrik.ma...@gmail.com</a>&gt;&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Hello,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Very often I would like to write something like this:\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;     data {\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] X;\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     parameters {\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] mu;\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] sigma;\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     model {\r<br>&gt;        X ~ normal(mu, sigma);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;        mu ~ normal(0, 1);\r<br>&gt;        sigma ~ inv_gamma(6, 5);\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     But end up explicitly looping:\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;     data {\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] X;\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     parameters {\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] mu;\r<br>&gt;        matrix[3,3] sigma;\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     model {\r<br>&gt;        for (row in 1:3) {\r<br>&gt;          for (col in 1:3) {\r<br>&gt;            X[row, col] ~ normal(mu[row,col], sigma[row,col]);\r<br>&gt;            mu[row, col] ~ normal(0, 1);\r<br>&gt;            sigma[row, col] ~ inv_gamma(6, 5);\r<br>&gt;          }\r<br>&gt;        }\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "You can rewrite the model here using one layer of vectorization:\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>|\r<br>model {\r<br>   for (row in 1:3) {\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "     X[row] ~ normal(mu[row], sigma[row]);\r<br>     mu[row] ~ normal(0, 1);\r<br>     sigma[row] ~ inv_gamma(6,5);\r<br>   }\r<br>}\r<br>|\r<br>\r<br>If you just supply a row index to a matrix, it picks out that row.\r<br>\r<br>If you&#39;re doing this, it&#39;s faster to represent the matrices as\r<br>arrays of vectors, which won&#39;t require copying and allocating (both\r<br>of which are cheap, by the way, compared to calling distribution\r<br>functions).\r<br>\r<br>In general, the loops should go the other way (column then row)\r<br>for speed because that&#39;s how memory is organized for matrices.\r<br>For arrays of vectors, it goes the other way around.\r<br>\r<br>These are all going to be fairly minor differences for matrices\r<br>this small, but the savings increase with the size of the matrix.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
